page_content_markdown:
   Home  Browse  Docs  Downloads  Releases  About    Documentation  Getting started  Use the SSA Ontology when you need a stable public identifier and definition for an SSA concept—in writing, research, software, AI systems, policy analysis, or evidence records.    For people    1. Find a concept  Search the  concept index  or browse a concept scheme.    2. Link the concept  Use the canonical page URL rather than copying an untraceable definition.    3. Attribute the source  Cite the SSA Ontology, version number, concept name, and canonical URI.      For software and AI systems  Each concept page contains a Schema.org  DefinedTerm  JSON-LD object. A system can identify a concept using its canonical URI:  {
  "@type": "DefinedTerm",
  "@id": "https://www.mcloughlin.world/glossaries/ssa-lexicon/term/academic-colonisation/",
  "name": "Academic Colonisation"
}  The ontology remains deliberately small and testable in v0.1.0. Hierarchical and causal assertions should only be added when there is enough evidence to govern them responsibly.    Preferred citation  McLoughlin, Sarah Ailish.  Strategic Self-Advocacy Ontology , version 0.1.0, 2026-07-29. https://www.mcloughlin.world/glossaries/ssa-lexicon/   SSA Ontology v0.1.0 · Created by Sarah Ailish McLoughlin · Ontology content licensed CC BY 4.0.

